<Project>
  <!--
    The sample's modules: a folder each, and in it a project per layer. Modules/Projects/Examples.Tenancy.Projects.Domain
    is a project of module Projects, because it is in the folder Projects. That is said once, here, for every project
    below: DDD_Module is the name of the folder a project's folder is in, and that is the module the project declares,
    as [assembly: Module("Projects")] would. No project has a file that says so, and a layer added to a module is that
    module's from its first build.

    So Projects' five projects, Tenants' five and Inspections' four are three modules. To the boundary analyzer, a
    project may name what the projects of its own module declare, and of another module only what that one publishes:
    the public types of its contracts project, its ids, keys and gates (DDD00022), and never its entities (DDD00023).
    Both are errors here, so the boundary is held and not only reported. To the generators, an infrastructure
    project is written the registrations its module's other projects need: the converters of their
    ids, their domain events under their names, and a package's registrations closed over their classes, such as
    modelBuilder.AddTenancy() in Tenants' and AddProjectMembershipWithTenancy in Projects'. Tenants' domain project,
    which declares Tenancy's classes, gets TenancyUseCases: Tenancy's use cases closed over those classes, under the
    package's own name for them, which every project above it names a use case and its records through, so none of
    them writes the nine types.

    The build writes the declaration into each project, and the toolkit's generator writes [assembly: Module] from it;
    see "A module named by its folder" in docs/modules.md. The host, the shared projects and the tests are outside this
    folder, and none of them is a module.

    A module's contracts project is said once here as well: a project whose name ends in .Contracts sets
    DDD_ModuleContracts, and the toolkit's generator writes [assembly: ModuleContracts] into it, so every public type
    of it is published and none carries [ModuleContract]. That condition on the name is this sample's own convention,
    not the toolkit's, which never takes a project for contracts because of its name; see "A contracts project" in
    docs/modules.md. A type a contracts project keeps to itself is internal.

    MSBuild reads the nearest Directory.Build.props only, so this one imports the repository's, which the projects
    below need as much as any other.
  -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <DDD_Module>$([System.IO.Path]::GetFileName($([System.IO.Path]::GetDirectoryName($(MSBuildProjectDirectory)))))</DDD_Module>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);DDD00022;DDD00023</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Contracts'))">
    <DDD_ModuleContracts>true</DDD_ModuleContracts>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
